sp_widget.client_script:
function CaseSubmissionWidgetController($scope, $http) { /* * Case Submission Widget - client-side AngularJS controller. * * Implements the case-submission user interaction described in * AAP Section 0.4.4 ("Page 1 - Case Submission: a simple form * with five inputs (subject, type, description, requester_name, * requester_email). On submit, calls the scripted REST endpoint, * displays a confirmation panel showing the returned case number * and a friendly 'Your case has been submitted' message."). * * Exposed properties (template-bound): * c.formData - object with five string keys (subject, type, * description, requester_name, requester_email). * Each key is initialized to '' (empty string). * Each key is bound to its corresponding form * input via ng-model. * c.submitting - boolean; true while the in-flight $http.post * request is pending. Drives the button's * ng-disabled and the "Submitting..." text. * c.errorMessage - string; non-empty when the most recent * submit attempt failed AT THE SERVER. Drives * the alert panel's ng-if="c.errorMessage". * Reset to '' at the start of every new submit * and by c.onInputChange(). * c.validationMessage * - string; non-empty when the most recent press * was REFUSED WITH THE OFFENDING FIELDS NAMED - * either client-side because the form was * invalid, or server-side by an HTTP 400 whose * body named them. Mutually exclusive with * c.errorMessage by construction: each path * sets one and clears the other. Cleared by * c.onInputChange() when the model actually * changes. * c.fieldErrors - object keyed by the FIVE public input field * names (subject, type, description, * requester_name, requester_email), each value * the endpoint's own caller-facing reason for * that field, taken from an HTTP 400 response * body's `fields` map and filtered by * c.acceptFieldErrors(). Empty object when the * most recent attempt produced no such map. * Drives each field's own message, has-error * class and aria-invalid alongside the * client-side validity state. * c.data - object provided by the Service Portal * framework, populated server-side by the * <script> body. Contains: * c.data.confirmationWidget - the embedded * confirmation widget instance loaded via * $sp.getWidget(). The success callback * mutates c.data.confirmationWidget.options. * number to pass the auto-generated case * number to the embedded widget. * c.data.submitted - boolean; flipped to * true after a successful POST. Drives * the conditional swap from the form panel * to the embedded confirmation widget panel. * c.data.submittedNumber - string; mirrors * the case number for any consumers that * want it directly off c.data (defense-in- * depth, in case the embedded widget's * options object is replaced rather than * mutated by some future refactor). * * Exposed methods (template-bound): * c.submit(form) - executes the case submission. Consults the * passed FormController FIRST and returns * without issuing any request when the form is * invalid, marking every control touched and * setting c.validationMessage. Otherwise issues * POST /api/x_casemgmt/case_submit with the * 5-field whitelisted payload via $http.post(). * On success (HTTP 201 with non-empty number), * sets c.data.submitted = true and mutates the * embedded confirmation widget's options.number * to the returned case number. On any error * (HTTP 4xx/5xx, network, missing number), * sets c.errorMessage to a generic, non-leaking * message so the verbatim alert is rendered. * The form MUST be passed as an argument - see * the guard inside for why $scope cannot supply * it. * c.onInputChange() * - bound to ng-change on all five inputs; drops * the previous attempt's outcome so at most one * answer is on screen at a time, but ONLY when * the model has actually changed since that * outcome was raised. See the function for the * ng-change firing that made the difference * load-bearing. * c.acceptFieldErrors(error) * - the whole of what this widget will accept from * a server error response: an HTTP 400, a plain * `fields` object, and only the five public * input keys with short string values. Returns * the filtered map, or null when the response * does not qualify - in which case the caller * falls back to the generic message and no * server text reaches the screen. * c.fieldInvalid(control, fieldName) * - true when a control is invalid AND Submit has * been pressed, OR the endpoint named that * field. The single condition behind each * field's has-error class, aria-invalid binding * and message. * c.clientInvalid(control) * - the client half of that condition on its own, * used to choose between this widget's own * wording and the endpoint's. * c.pressed - true once Submit has been pressed. Gates every * per-field message. Replaced the per-control * $touched flag, whose blur trigger moved the * button between mousedown and mouseup and so * swallowed the first press. * c.clientProblemRemains() * - true while the model still holds something * this widget would refuse to send. Keeps the * refusal summary on screen across an edit that * leaves another field flagged. * c.markAllTouched(form) * - marks every registered control $touched, so * AngularJS's own ng-touched state matches the * press. No longer what reveals the messages * (c.pressed is), but kept so the framework's * view of the form stays consistent with it. * c.requiredIncomplete() * - true while any of the four MANDATORY fields is * empty; lets the status region name the actual * blocking reason rather than assuming one. * * Design rationale: * - controller-as syntax (`var c = this`) per AAP Section 0.5.1's * widget-authoring conventions and per the sibling lookup widget * at ./sp_widget_x_casemgmt_case_lookup_widget.xml. * - $http (NOT fetch): Service Portal widgets run in AngularJS * 1.5.x context; $http is the canonical HTTP client. fetch() * would bypass AngularJS's digest cycle and require manual * $scope.$apply() handling. * - Strict 5-key payload (defense-in-depth): the POST body is * constructed by enumerating exactly the five whitelisted * fields, NOT by passing c.formData wholesale. Even if a future * refactor accidentally adds a sixth key to c.formData, the * POST body would still contain exactly the five whitelisted * fields. The server-side Script Include also drops any * non-whitelisted keys as a fallback, but the client-side * enumeration provides the first line of defense. * - Generic error message in template, NOT echoed from server: * the message "Submission failed. Please try again." is * hardcoded in this controller via direct assignment to * c.errorMessage. This controller deliberately does NOT pass * error.data.error or error.statusText to the template - the * server's error body is NEVER trusted by the client. This * matches the information-leakage hardening pattern used by * the sibling lookup widget. * - Identical handling of all HTTP errors: information-leakage * hardening. 400 (validation), 500 (server-error), 0 (network), * and "missing number on HTTP 200" all set the same generic * error. An attacker cannot distinguish "validation failed" * from "server error" from "network down" via the UX. * - c.submitting flag: prevents double-submit race conditions if * the user clicks the button twice quickly OR presses Enter * inside an input field while a request is in flight. The * ng-disabled binding on the button is the primary guard; * the early-return inside c.submit() is the defense-in-depth * fallback in case the disabled state is bypassed by a * keyboard event in an edge browser. * - String() coercion on the unwrapped body.number: ensures the * embedded widget's c.options.number is always a string, even * if the REST operation accidentally returns a number primitive. * Matches the embedded widget's own defensive coercion in * ./sp_widget_x_casemgmt_case_confirmation_widget.xml. */ var c = this;
// Initialize the form state with empty values for each whitelisted field. // The whitelist (subject, type, description, requester_name, // requester_email) matches exactly the fields accepted by // /api/x_casemgmt/case_submit per AAP Section 0.4.4 and 0.7.4. c.formData = { subject: '', type: '', description: '', requester_name: '', requester_email: '' };
// Submission state flags. Initialized with safe defaults so the // template's ng-if and ng-disabled expressions evaluate cleanly // even before c.submit() runs. c.submitting = false; c.errorMessage = '';
/* * The FIVE public input field names, in the order the form presents * them, used for three separate jobs: filtering the endpoint's field * errors, snapshotting the model to detect a real edit, and mapping a * server field name back to a control. * * It is exactly the whitelist the POST body carries and exactly the * set of keys x_casemgmt.CasePortalService can name in a validation * response. Keeping one list means the two can never drift apart in * this file. */ var PUBLIC_FIELDS = ['subject', 'type', 'description', 'requester_name', 'requester_email'];
/* * The longest server-supplied field message this widget will render. * * The endpoint's own messages are far shorter than this - the longest * authored one names a length limit and the length received, about 70 * characters, and the choice-list message grows with the number of * live `type` choices. The cap exists for the case this widget cannot * verify: a response that did not come from the endpoint. An * unbounded string from a hostile or broken responder would otherwise * be painted into the page at whatever length it arrived, and a * megabyte of text in a role="alert" region is a denial of the form * rather than a message about it. 300 characters leaves the real * messages untouched with room for a longer choice list. */ var MAX_FIELD_MESSAGE_LENGTH = 300;
/* * Field-level reasons returned by the endpoint for the most recent * refused submission, keyed by the public field name. * * ISSUE 14. The endpoint has always answered a malformed submission * with an actionable, per-field body - measured against the live * endpoint with requester_email "a@b": * HTTP 400 * {"result":{"error":"The submission could not be accepted because * one or more fields are invalid.","fields":{"requester_email": * "Email must be a valid address."}}} * and this controller discarded ALL of it, painting only "Submission * failed. Please try again." The requester was told that something * went wrong, was not told which of the five fields to look at, and * had no way to discover it: the form had already passed its own * checks, so nothing on screen was marked. * * The map is now consumed, under the strict acceptance rule in * c.acceptFieldErrors(). Rendering these five strings leaks nothing: * they are the endpoint's OWN authored, caller-facing contract about * inputs the public form already publishes - the field names are in * the widget source and the length limits are HTML attributes on the * controls - and no internal field name can appear in the map, * because the Script Include only ever puts those five keys in it. */ c.fieldErrors = {};
/* * Snapshot of the five model values taken at the moment an outcome * (summary, generic failure or field errors) was raised, or null when * no outcome is on screen. c.onInputChange() compares against it to * decide whether an ng-change firing represents a real edit. */ var outcomeSnapshot = null;
/** * The exact email rule the SERVER applies, mirrored on the client. * * ISSUE 14, first half. The email control carried type="email" and * nothing else, so the only client-side rule was AngularJS's built-in * EMAIL_REGEXP, which accepts an address with an undotted domain. * "a@b" therefore passed every client check, the press was allowed * through, and the endpoint refused it - because * x_casemgmt.CasePortalService._validateSubmission() tests * /^[^\s@]+@[^\s@.]+(\.[^\s@.]+)+$/ * which requires at least one dot in the domain. Client and server * disagreed, and the disagreement surfaced as a generic failure on a * form showing no error at all. * * This is that same expression, character for character, bound to the * control through ng-pattern. It is held here rather than inline in * the template so there is exactly one copy to keep in step with the * Script Include, and so this comment can name the file it mirrors: * ../../script_includes/x_casemgmt_CasePortalService.xml, in * _validateSubmission's requester_email branch. * * type="email" is DELIBERATELY KEPT alongside it. The attribute is * what gives a phone the @-bearing keyboard and what tells assistive * technology the field's purpose, and Angular applies both validators * so the effective client rule is their intersection. That * intersection is stricter than the server only for addresses * Angular's own validator already rejected before this change (its * domain-label rule refuses "a@-b.c", which this pattern would * allow), so nothing that used to be submittable stopped being * submittable - and any residual disagreement now paints the * endpoint's own field message rather than a generic failure. */ c.emailPattern = /^[^\s@]+@[^\s@.]+(\.[^\s@.]+)+$/;
/** * True once Submit has been pressed at least once on this form. * * This flag, and not the per-control $touched flag, is what reveals a * per-field message. The distinction is not cosmetic - it decides * whether the FIRST press of Submit does anything at all. * * $touched flips on blur. A pointer press on Submit blurs the control * the requester was in, so with visibility keyed to $touched the * sequence was: mousedown -> blur -> $setTouched -> a role="alert" * paragraph is inserted above the button -> the button moves ~20px * down -> mouseup lands on the <form> in the gap the button just * vacated -> ng-submit never fires. Measured on the rendered page: * after that press caseSubmitForm.$submitted was still false and * c.validationMessage was still empty while the per-field message was * already on screen, i.e. the requester saw their first click do * nothing and had to press again. Reserving the space with CSS would * also close it, but AAP Section 0.4.4 fixes this portal to the * platform default theme with no custom CSS, so the fix is to stop * the blur from changing the layout. * * Keying on the press instead means blur changes nothing on screen - * validity itself is already current, because AngularJS revalidates * on input rather than on blur - so no layout moves between mousedown * and mouseup and the first press is always delivered. After that * first press the flag stays true, so messages then track the model * live: a field's message clears as it is corrected and appears as it * is broken, which is the feedback the requester needs at the point * they are acting on it. */ c.pressed = false;
/** * Capture the current model values as the baseline for "has anything * actually changed since we answered the press?". */ function captureOutcomeSnapshot() { var snapshot = {}; for (var i = 0; i < PUBLIC_FIELDS.length; i++) { snapshot[PUBLIC_FIELDS[i]] = c.formData[PUBLIC_FIELDS[i]]; } outcomeSnapshot = snapshot; }
/* * Message raised when the requester presses Submit while the form is * not yet valid. * * This exists because Submit is no longer disabled. It used to be * ng-disabled on caseSubmitForm.$invalid, which produced two distinct * dead ends. A disabled <button> is pointer-transparent, so a click * aimed at it landed on the surrounding DIV.form-group and NOTHING * happened - no message, no focus move, not even the declared * cursor:not-allowed, which can never render on a control that does * not receive the pointer. And a disabled button is removed from the * tab order, so a keyboard user tabbing through the form never reached * the primary action at all and had no way to discover why. * * Keeping it enabled and validating on click inverts that: the press * is always answered. c.submit() marks every control touched, which * is what makes each field's own role="alert" message render, and sets * this summary so the answer is visible at the button too rather than * only beside whichever field is off-screen. * * It is a SUMMARY, not a substitute: the per-field messages say which * field and why, and this says that the press was received and refused. */ c.validationMessage = '';
/** * Discard the outcome of the previous attempt - but only once the * requester has actually changed something. * * Bound to ng-change on every input. Without it, c.errorMessage * survived while the requester edited the form, so a failed * submission's "Submission failed. Please try again." alert sat above * fields the requester was already fixing, and could coexist with a * fresh validation summary and a per-field message - up to three * messages describing three different moments at once. An edit is * evidence that the requester has moved on from that outcome, so the * outcome goes. * * ISSUE 14, THE SECOND HALF: WHY "AN EDIT" AND NOT "A KEYSTROKE". * * This function used to clear unconditionally, and QA measured the * consequence: on a whitespace-only form, the first press raised the * summary and then the summary disappeared while the per-field * messages stayed - a form showing four field errors and no statement * that the press had been refused, which reads as though the press * was never received. * * The mechanism is that ng-change does not mean "the value changed". * AngularJS's input directive applies ng-trim BEFORE $setViewValue, * so typing a space into an empty control commits a view value of '' * - unchanged - and $commitViewValue's early return is skipped for a * control with NATIVE validators (type="email" has them), so the * view-change listeners fire and this function ran on a model that * had not moved. A press followed by a stray space in the email * field therefore cleared the answer to that press. * * The snapshot comparison below is the fix: the outcome is dropped * only when at least one of the five model values differs from what * it was when the outcome was raised. The summary is now present * whenever a press has been refused and is removed only by a real * change, which is the invariant the defect violated. * * Per-field CLIENT messages are deliberately not touched here: they * are bound to live $invalid && c.pressed state and correct themselves * the instant the field becomes valid. Per-field SERVER messages * cannot self-correct - they are a statement about a value already * sent - so each one is dropped individually, and only for the field * whose value moved. A message about an unedited field is still true * and stays on screen. */ c.onInputChange = function() { if (!outcomeSnapshot) { // Nothing is on screen to invalidate. This is the ordinary // case: the requester is filling the form in for the first // time and no press has been answered yet. return; } var changed = false; for (var i = 0; i < PUBLIC_FIELDS.length; i++) { var field = PUBLIC_FIELDS[i]; if (c.formData[field] === outcomeSnapshot[field]) { continue; } changed = true; outcomeSnapshot[field] = c.formData[field]; if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(c.fieldErrors, field)) { delete c.fieldErrors[field]; } } if (!changed) { return; } // The generic failure banner is press-scoped: it describes one // transport-level attempt ("Submission failed. Please try again.") // and a real edit supersedes that attempt, so it goes. c.errorMessage = ''; // The refusal summary is NOT press-scoped in the same way. Its // text points at the fields still marked above it - "Correct the // highlighted fields above, then choose Submit again" - so while // any of them is still marked that sentence is still true, and it // is the only page-level statement that the case has NOT been // submitted. Clearing it on any edit withdrew that statement while // marked fields and their role="alert" messages were still on // screen. Measured on the rendered page: one keystroke in Email // removed the summary while "Subject is required." stayed beside // an invalid Subject, and the same keystroke removed it while the // endpoint's own "Email must be a valid address." stayed beside // Email after a genuine HTTP 400. So the summary now survives an // edit that leaves a problem behind, and is withdrawn only once // the form would be accepted. if (c.hasFieldErrors() || c.clientProblemRemains()) { return; } c.validationMessage = ''; outcomeSnapshot = null; };
/** * True while any field-level server message is still on screen. * * Used to decide whether the snapshot must be kept: while a message * about an unedited field survives, so must the baseline that lets a * later edit clear it. * * @returns {boolean} true when c.fieldErrors holds at least one key. */ c.hasFieldErrors = function() { for (var key in c.fieldErrors) { if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(c.fieldErrors, key)) { return true; } } return false; };
/** * The client half of the per-field invalid condition. * * $invalid && c.pressed, guarded against a control that has not * registered yet (the form lives under an ng-if, so on the very first * digest the named controls can be undefined). * * The second half was $touched until this change. See c.pressed for * why it is now the press and not the blur: keying on blur inserted a * message between mousedown and mouseup and moved the button out from * under the pointer, so the first press of Submit was swallowed. * * @param {Object} control - an ngModel controller off the form. * @returns {boolean} true when the control is invalid and Submit has * been pressed at least once. */ c.clientInvalid = function(control) { return !!(control && control.$invalid && c.pressed); };
/** * The whole per-field invalid condition: this widget's own validation * OR the endpoint's. * * Both halves must drive the same three things - the has-error class, * the aria-invalid binding and the message element - or the field * would be described as invalid by one channel and valid by another. * A server field error with no red border and aria-invalid="false" * would be exactly that: a message next to a control the page insists * is fine. * * @param {Object} control - an ngModel controller off the form. * @param {String} fieldName - the public field name (snake_case), as * the endpoint names it. * @returns {boolean} true when either channel reports the field wrong. */ c.fieldInvalid = function(control, fieldName) { if (c.clientInvalid(control)) { return true; } return !!(fieldName && Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(c.fieldErrors, fieldName)); };
/** * Decide what, if anything, may be taken from a server error response * and shown to the requester. * * THIS FUNCTION IS THE INFORMATION-LEAKAGE PERIMETER. The hardening * described at length in this record's header - "the server's HTTP * 4xx/5xx body is NEVER trusted by the client" - is preserved in * substance by narrowing what "trusted" means to a shape that cannot * carry anything but the endpoint's own published input contract. * Every one of these conditions is load-bearing: * * 1. HTTP 400 ONLY. A 400 is the status the submit operation * answers when, and only when, x_casemgmt.CasePortalService * refused the PAYLOAD. Every other status is either not about * the payload or not from that endpoint's own code: 429 is the * flood guard, 401/403 are authorization, 404/405 are routing, * 415 is the content type, 5xx is the platform, and 0 is the * network. Measured against the live instance, the platform's * own 405 and 415 bodies are shaped * {"error":{"message":"...","detail":null},"status":"failure"} * - an `error` OBJECT, not a string, and no `fields` at all - so * they are refused twice over, by status and by shape. * * 2. A `fields` OBJECT, not an array and not a string. An array * would let a responder push positional text; a string would let * it push a single unfiltered sentence. * * 3. ONLY the five public input keys. Any other key - `status`, * `assigned_agent`, `sys_id`, anything invented - is dropped * silently. The Script Include cannot produce such a key, so * this only matters for a response that did not come from it, * which is precisely the case worth defending. * * 4. String values only, non-empty after trimming, capped at * MAX_FIELD_MESSAGE_LENGTH. * * 5. If nothing survives, return null so the caller paints the * generic message. A 400 whose `fields` map names only internal * keys is indistinguishable, at the UX, from a 500. * * The values that DO survive are rendered through AngularJS * interpolation ({{ }}), which HTML-escapes, and never through * ng-bind-html, so markup in a message cannot execute. * * The `result` unwrap mirrors the success path: a ServiceNow Scripted * REST API wraps whatever the operation script passes to * response.setBody() in a `result` envelope, so the map arrives at * error.data.result.fields - measured - while a caller that suppressed * the envelope would see error.data.fields. Both are read. * * @param {Object} error - the $http rejection object. * @returns {Object|null} a filtered field-name -> message map with at * least one entry, or null when this response may not contribute * any text to the page. */ c.acceptFieldErrors = function(error) { if (!error || Number(error.status) !== 400) { return null; } var body = (error.data && error.data.result) ? error.data.result : error.data; if (!body || typeof body !== 'object') { return null; } var fields = body.fields; if (!fields || typeof fields !== 'object' || Array.isArray(fields)) { return null; } var accepted = {}; var acceptedCount = 0; for (var i = 0; i < PUBLIC_FIELDS.length; i++) { var field = PUBLIC_FIELDS[i]; if (!Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(fields, field)) { continue; } var message = fields[field]; if (typeof message !== 'string') { continue; } message = message.trim(); if (message === '') { continue; } if (message.length > MAX_FIELD_MESSAGE_LENGTH) { message = message.substring(0, MAX_FIELD_MESSAGE_LENGTH); } accepted[field] = message; acceptedCount++; } return acceptedCount > 0 ? accepted : null; };
/** * Mark every registered control on a form as touched. * * The per-field messages and the red has-error treatment are bound to * $invalid && c.pressed (see c.pressed for why the second half is the * press rather than the blur it used to be), so this walk is no longer * what makes them appear. It is kept because AngularJS's own * ng-touched / ng-untouched classes and $touched state are part of the * framework's public view of the form, and leaving a pressed form * describing its controls as never-visited would be inconsistent with * what the page is showing. $setSubmitted() does not cover it - it * sets $submitted on the FORM, not $touched on the controls - so the * controls are walked explicitly. * * The walk tests for the $setTouched function rather than filtering by * name, because a form controller's own properties ($valid, $error, * $name and so on) are mixed in with its controls under the same * object; possessing $setTouched is what distinguishes an ngModel * controller from form bookkeeping, and it is also the guard that * keeps this from throwing on a future AngularJS whose form * controller exposes different internals. * * @param {Object} form - the AngularJS form controller. */ c.markAllTouched = function(form) { if (!form) { return; } if (typeof form.$setSubmitted === 'function') { form.$setSubmitted(); } for (var key in form) { if (key.charAt(0) === '$') { continue; } var control = form[key]; if (control && typeof control.$setTouched === 'function') { control.$setTouched(); } } };
/** * True while any of the FOUR MANDATORY fields is still empty. * * This exists so the status region under the Submit button can name the * ACTUAL reason a press would be refused instead of asserting one. * * c.submit() refuses the press whenever the form has any invalid control, * and the form has five controls of which only four are mandatory. The * fifth, requester_email, is OPTIONAL but still format-checked by * type="email", so a half-typed address such as "abc@" makes the form * invalid and blocks the press while every mandatory field is complete. * The region previously read "Every field marked * is required. Complete * all four to enable Submit." in that state, which is false twice over: * all four ARE complete, and the field actually blocking the submission is * not marked with an asterisk at all. A requester following that * instruction re-reads the four starred fields, finds nothing wrong, and * has no way forward. * * (The wording "the button is disabled" was accurate when the button was * ng-disabled on form validity. It no longer is - the button is disabled * only while a request is in flight, and validity is decided on the press * - but this function is needed for exactly the same reason, because the * region still has to say which of the two blocking conditions holds.) * * Splitting the reason in two needs a test for "a mandatory field is * empty" that is independent of the email's format validity, which is * what this function is. It reads c.formData rather than the form * controller because emptiness is the whole of what `required` means * here, and because AngularJS trims text input into the model by default * (ng-trim), so a whitespace-only entry arrives here as '' exactly as it * arrives at the required validator. The two therefore agree in every * state, and the three status messages are mutually exclusive and * exhaustive: a mandatory field is empty, or the optional email is * malformed, or the form is ready to submit. * * @returns {boolean} true when subject, type, description or * requester_name is empty; false when all four are populated. */ c.requiredIncomplete = function() { return !c.formData.subject || !c.formData.type || !c.formData.description || !c.formData.requester_name; };
/** * True while the model still holds something this widget would refuse * to send: a blank mandatory field, or an address that fails the * server's own regular expression. * * This is the client-side half of "is there still a problem on this * form?", and c.onInputChange() uses it to decide whether the refusal * summary may be withdrawn. It is computed from c.formData rather than * from the FormController because onInputChange is an ng-change * handler with no form argument to read - and the two agree by * construction, since the same four values carry `required` in the * template and the same regular expression is bound there as * ng-pattern. * * @returns {boolean} true when a press would still be refused on the * client. */ c.clientProblemRemains = function() { if (c.requiredIncomplete()) { return true; } var email = c.formData.requester_email; return !!(email && !c.emailPattern.test(email)); };
/** * Submit the form data to the scripted REST endpoint at * POST /api/x_casemgmt/case_submit. * * Per AAP Section 0.4.4: posts EXACTLY the five whitelisted fields * (subject, type, description, requester_name, requester_email). * No additional fields are sent. * * Per AAP Section 0.4.4: on success, flips c.data.submitted to true * and passes the returned case number to the embedded confirmation * widget via c.data.confirmationWidget.options.number. The embedded * widget renders the verbatim message "Your case has been submitted" * plus the case number. * * Per AAP Section 0.7.2 (information-leakage hardening): on any * error (HTTP 4xx/5xx, network, missing number on HTTP 200), sets * c.errorMessage to the generic string "Submission failed. Please * try again." This function MUST NOT echo error.data.error, * error.statusText, or any server-supplied text. * * @param {Object} [submittedForm] - the caseSubmitForm FormController, * passed by the template's ng-submit. REQUIRED in practice for * the validation guard to run: the form lives under an ng-if, so * its controller is published on a child scope and is NOT * reachable as $scope.caseSubmitForm. See the guard for the * measurement. */ c.submit = function(submittedForm) { // Record that a press has been delivered. This is the flag every // per-field message is keyed to (see c.pressed), and it is set // here - first statement, before any branch - so that a press // answered by the guard below and a press that reaches the // endpoint both reveal the messages that explain them. c.pressed = true; // Idempotent guard: prevent double-submit if the user clicks // the button or presses Enter while a request is in flight. // The button is also disabled in this case via ng-disabled, // but the guard provides defense-in-depth in case the disabled // state is bypassed by an edge browser's keyboard handling. if (c.submitting) { return; }
// Validate on click, because the button is no longer disabled // while the form is invalid. // // The button being enabled is what makes the press answerable at // all (a disabled button is pointer-transparent and out of the tab // order - see c.validationMessage), and this branch is what // answers it. Marking every control touched flips each field's own // role="alert" message into the DOM, so the requester is told which // fields are wrong and why, and the summary states that the press // was received and refused. // // NOTHING is sent in this branch: the early return happens before // any $http.post, so an incomplete payload never reaches the // endpoint, exactly as the disabled button previously ensured. The // server-side guarantee is unchanged either way - // x_casemgmt.CasePortalService._validateSubmission() refuses a // payload with an empty mandatory field or a malformed address with // an HTTP 400 - so this is a UX layer over an enforced contract, // not the enforcement itself. // // The FormController is passed IN by the template, not read off // $scope, and that is load-bearing rather than stylistic. // // The <form> lives inside <div ng-if="!c.data.submitted">, and // ng-if creates a CHILD scope. AngularJS's form directive // publishes the FormController on the scope where it links - the // child - so $scope.caseSubmitForm, read on the controller's own // scope, is undefined. Measured on the rendered page: the form // element's scope was $id 25 with caseSubmitForm as an own // property ($invalid true), while the controller's scope was its // parent $id 24 where hasOwnProperty('caseSubmitForm') was false. // // The consequence was worse than no validation: with `form` // undefined the guard below was skipped entirely, so a press on a // pristine form fell straight through to $http.post, sent // {"subject":"","type":"","description":"","requester_name":"", // "requester_email":""}, took the endpoint's HTTP 400, and painted // the generic "Submission failed. Please try again." - naming no // field, marking nothing, and logging a console error for a // request that should never have left the browser. // // ng-submit="c.submit(caseSubmitForm)" evaluates the expression in // the CHILD scope, where the name does resolve, and hands the // controller in. The $scope fallback is kept for the case where // this method is called without an argument (a keyboard path or a // future caller), and it is correct there because a caller with no // form to pass is one whose scope is the only source available. var form = submittedForm || $scope.caseSubmitForm; if (form && form.$invalid) { c.markAllTouched(form); c.errorMessage = ''; // The endpoint's field messages, if any survive from an // earlier attempt, are about values that have since been // re-validated on the client. The client's own per-field // messages are the current answer, so the server's are // dropped to keep one statement per field. c.fieldErrors = {}; c.validationMessage = 'Your case has not been submitted yet. Correct the highlighted fields above, then choose Submit again.'; // Baseline for c.onInputChange(): the summary stays until one // of these five values actually changes. captureOutcomeSnapshot(); return; }
// Reset state at the start of every submit. The error message, // the summary, the endpoint's field messages and the submitting // flag drive mutually-exclusive UI affordances, and the outcome // snapshot goes with them - there is no outcome on screen while a // request is in flight. c.submitting = true; c.errorMessage = ''; c.validationMessage = ''; c.fieldErrors = {}; outcomeSnapshot = null;
// POST to the scripted REST endpoint. The payload is built by // enumerating EXACTLY the five whitelisted fields - NOT by // passing c.formData wholesale - so any future expansion of // c.formData does not accidentally widen the payload surface. // This is defense-in-depth alongside the server-side whitelist // enforced by x_casemgmt.CasePortalService.submitCase(). $http.post('/api/x_casemgmt/case_submit', { subject: c.formData.subject, type: c.formData.type, description: c.formData.description, requester_name: c.formData.requester_name, requester_email: c.formData.requester_email }).then(function(response) { // Success path (HTTP 2xx). The server's REST operation // returns { number, message } per its contract - but a // ServiceNow Scripted REST API wraps whatever the operation // script passes to response.setBody() inside a `result` // envelope before it reaches the wire. Measured against the // live endpoint: the 201 body is // {"result":{"number":"CASE0001126","message":"Your case has been submitted"}} // so the number lives at response.data.result.number, and // reading response.data.number yields undefined. That single // mis-read is what previously drove a fully successful // submission (HTTP 201, case created in Draft) into the else // branch below, showing the requester "Submission failed. // Please try again." while the case existed - the worst // possible outcome, because the natural reaction is to press // Submit again and create duplicates. // // The unwrap below prefers the envelope and falls back to the // bare body, so it is correct whether or not the envelope is // applied (the platform can be configured either way, and a // caller may append sysparm_no_envelope). c.submitting = false; var body = (response && response.data && response.data.result) ? response.data.result : (response ? response.data : null); if (body && body.number) { // Pass the returned case number to the embedded // confirmation widget by mutating its options.number. // The embedded widget's controller assigns // c.options = $scope.options; // so this mutation propagates reactively through // Angular's two-way binding. if (c.data.confirmationWidget && c.data.confirmationWidget.options) { c.data.confirmationWidget.options.number = String(body.number); } // Mirror the case number on c.data for any consumer that // wants it directly. Defense-in-depth in case the // embedded widget's options object is replaced rather // than mutated by some future refactor. c.data.submittedNumber = String(body.number); // Flip the submitted flag - the template swaps from // the form panel to the embedded confirmation widget. c.data.submitted = true; } else { // Defensive: HTTP 200 but no case number in the body. // This should never happen if the REST operation // conforms to its contract (it always returns either // { number, message } on success or { error } on // failure), but the guard prevents a confusing UX // where the form vanishes without a confirmation. c.errorMessage = 'Submission failed. Please try again.'; captureOutcomeSnapshot(); } }, function(error) { // Error path (HTTP 4xx, 5xx, or network failure / 0). // // There are now exactly TWO outcomes here, and only the // first renders any server-supplied text: // // (a) an HTTP 400 carrying the endpoint's own `fields` // map, filtered by c.acceptFieldErrors() down to the // five public input keys. Each surviving message is // rendered beside its own control, and the summary // states that the press was refused - the same // sentence the client-side refusal uses, because it // states the same fact: nothing was submitted, and // the fields to fix are marked. This is Issue 14: // previously this response painted "Submission // failed. Please try again." and nothing else, on a // form that had passed its own checks and therefore // marked nothing at all, so the requester was told // that something was wrong and never which field. // // (b) EVERYTHING else - 401, 403, 404, 405, 415, 429, any // 5xx, a network failure, status 0, and a 400 whose // map yields nothing acceptable - takes the generic, // hardcoded message with NO server text whatsoever, // exactly as before. An attacker probing the endpoint // still cannot distinguish those outcomes from one // another through the UX, and error.data.error and // error.statusText are still never displayed. // // No console.log() in either branch: the response body is // not written to the browser console, so the forensics // surface is unchanged too. c.submitting = false; var accepted = c.acceptFieldErrors(error); if (accepted) { c.fieldErrors = accepted; c.errorMessage = ''; c.validationMessage = 'Your case has not been submitted yet. Correct the highlighted fields above, then choose Submit again.'; } else { c.fieldErrors = {}; c.validationMessage = ''; c.errorMessage = 'Submission failed. Please try again.'; } // Baseline for c.onInputChange(): whichever answer is now on // screen stays until one of the five values actually moves. captureOutcomeSnapshot(); }); }; }
